4. Internal Working of ConcurrentHashMap.

1. ConcurrentHashMap stores data using an internal hash table, similar to HashMap.
2. In Java 8+, it uses CAS (Compare-And-Swap) for certain updates and synchronized locking at the individual bucket/bin level when needed.
3. Multiple threads can read and update different buckets concurrently, reducing contention.
4. It uses volatile reads and internal synchronization for visibility and thread safety. It does not allow null keys or null values.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

5. Difference Between Hashtable and HashMap.

1. HashMap is not synchronized, whereas Hashtable synchronizes its methods.
2. HashMap allows one null key and multiple null values; Hashtable does not allow null keys or values.
3. HashMap generally provides better performance in single-threaded use because it avoids synchronization overhead.
4. For concurrent applications, prefer ConcurrentHashMap over Hashtable because it offers better concurrency.

