18. What Is RAG? Explain Its Use Cases.

1. RAG stands for Retrieval-Augmented Generation. It combines information retrieval with an LLM to generate context-aware answers.
2. First, documents are split into chunks and converted into embeddings, which are stored in a vector database.
3. When a user asks a question, the system retrieves relevant chunks and sends them as context to the LLM.
4. RAG is useful for company knowledge assistants, document search, customer support, and healthcare information retrieval.

Example – Healthcare:

    Medical Documents
          |
          v
       Chunking
          |
          v
       Embeddings
          |
          v
      Vector Database
          |
          v
      User Question
          |
          v
    Retrieve Relevant Data
          |
          v
        LLM
          |
          v
